= Syntax — Technical =

== Representational choices ==
Syntactic frameworks differ over whether structure is best modeled through constituency, dependency, feature structures, constructions, derivations, constraints, or combinations.

A constituency analysis may represent hierarchical phrase structure:

<pre>
S
├─ NP
└─ VP
   ├─ V
   └─ NP
</pre>

A dependency analysis instead centers directed head–dependent relations among lexical or functional elements.

== Long-distance dependency ==
Phenomena such as interrogatives, relativization, control, raising, binding, and agreement motivate representations that can connect non-adjacent material.

Frameworks disagree over whether these involve movement, feature propagation, lexical constraints, constructional pairings, dependency graphs, or other mechanisms.

== Syntax–semantics interface ==
A formal grammar may pair a syntactic derivation or structure with semantic composition:

<pre>
syntactic structure
      ↓ interpretation rules
semantic representation
</pre>

The mapping need not be one-to-one. Scope ambiguity, ellipsis, anaphora, coercion, and information structure can require additional machinery.

== Evidence ==
Syntactic evidence includes:

* acceptability judgments;
* corpus distributions;
* cross-linguistic comparison;
* experimental processing data;
* acquisition patterns;
* typological constraints;
* computational parsing performance.

No single evidence source directly reveals an invisible syntactic structure without theoretical assumptions.

== Formal-language contrast ==
In a formal language, syntax can be specified extensionally or recursively by explicit formation rules. Natural-language grammars are empirical models inferred from variable human behavior and cross-linguistic evidence.

